Fix use-after-free in tlsProcessPendingData() pending-list iteration

tlsProcessPendingData() walked pending_list with a listIter, which caches the
next node pointer. The read handler run by tlsHandleEvent() can execute a
command (e.g. CLIENT KILL) that closes another pending TLS connection, whose
connTLSClose() frees its pending_list node; the cached pointer then dangles
and the following listNext() reads freed memory (SIGSEGV). Replace the
iteration with a bounded detach-from-head drain that never holds a list node
pointer across a handler call.

Tracker: none
Upstream patch: https://github.com/redis/redis/commit/b12c4db14a5e3dba28f431cc8863951eb93eb663
Adaptation: src/tls.c -> src/tls.cpp; kept KeyDB's serverAssert(!GlobalLocksAcquired()) at the top of the function and the explicit (tls_connection*) cast of listNodeValue() required by C++. The list is thread_local in KeyDB, but a kill issued on the owning thread still deletes a sibling node mid-iteration, so the fix logic is identical.

diff --git a/src/tls.cpp b/src/tls.cpp
--- a/src/tls.cpp
+++ b/src/tls.cpp
@@ -1290,14 +1290,15 @@ int tlsHasPendingData() {
 }
 
 int tlsProcessPendingData() {
-    listIter li;
-    listNode *ln;
     serverAssert(!GlobalLocksAcquired());
 
     int processed = listLength(pending_list);
-    listRewind(pending_list,&li);
-    while((ln = listNext(&li))) {
+    for (int i = 0; i < processed; i++) {
+        listNode *ln = listFirst(pending_list);
+        if (!ln) break;
         tls_connection *conn = (tls_connection*)listNodeValue(ln);
+        listDelNode(pending_list, conn->pending_list_node);
+        conn->pending_list_node = NULL;
         tlsHandleEvent(conn, AE_READABLE);
     }
     return processed;
